{"content_hash": "4e24e91115db1d2c", "version_id": 231, "article_id": 24, "level_code": "en_a1", "lang": "en", "level": 1, "level_label": "A1 入门", "title": "Plague Is Still Here: Cases in Africa and US", "title_zh": "鼠疫还没消失：非洲和美国每年都有病例", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2026/the-plague-never-went.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-08", "word_count": 182, "reading_minutes": 0.9, "lead": "The plague is a very old disease. Many people think it is gone. But it is still here today.", "lead_zh": "鼠疫是一种非常古老的疾病。很多人以为它已经消失了。但它今天仍然存在。", "preview": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. It never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with medicine. Most cases are in Africa. C", "preview_zh": "很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。今天，医生可以用药物治疗它。大多数病例发生在非洲。刚果和马达加斯加有很多病例。美国每年大约有七例。大多数发生在西部的农村地区。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 222, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/audio/24/en_a1.mp3", "duration": 80.888, "size_bytes": 489024, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 213, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/data/versions/231.json", "paragraphs": ["The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. It never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with medicine. Most cases are in Africa. Congo and Madagascar have many cases. The United States has about seven cases every year. Most are in the rural West.", "A bacteria called Yersinia pestis causes the plague. Rodents like rats and prairie dogs carry it. Fleas bite the animals. Then the fleas bite people. People can also get sick from sick animals. Bubonic plague is the most common form. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen.", "There are two other forms. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. Both are very dangerous without early treatment. Bubonic plague can become these forms if people do not get help. A person with pneumonic plague can spread it by coughing.", "Antibiotics can cure the plague. People must see a doctor fast. A vaccine exists, but the WHO only gives it to some workers. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S. Recently, a worker in Siberia died. The institute is now under quarantine."], "paragraphs_zh": ["很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。今天，医生可以用药物治疗它。大多数病例发生在非洲。刚果和马达加斯加有很多病例。美国每年大约有七例。大多数发生在西部的农村地区。", "一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌会引起鼠疫。老鼠和草原犬鼠等啮齿动物携带这种细菌。跳蚤叮咬这些动物，然后跳蚤再叮咬人。人们也可能因为接触生病的动物而生病。腺鼠疫是最常见的形式。它会让淋巴结非常疼痛和肿胀。", "还有另外两种形式。肺鼠疫感染肺部。败血型鼠疫感染血液。如果不尽早治疗，这两种都非常危险。如果人们不寻求帮助，腺鼠疫可能会变成这两种形式。肺鼠疫患者可以通过咳嗽传播疾病。", "抗生素可以治愈鼠疫。人们必须尽快去看医生。有疫苗，但世界卫生组织只给一些工作人员接种。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。最近，西伯利亚一名工作人员死亡。该研究所现在被隔离。"], "body": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. It never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with medicine. Most cases are in Africa. Congo and Madagascar have many cases. The United States has about seven cases every year. Most are in the rural West.\n\nA bacteria called Yersinia pestis causes the plague. Rodents like rats and prairie dogs carry it. Fleas bite the animals. Then the fleas bite people. People can also get sick from sick animals. Bubonic plague is the most common form. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen.\n\nThere are two other forms. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. Both are very dangerous without early treatment. Bubonic plague can become these forms if people do not get help. A person with pneumonic plague can spread it by coughing.\n\nAntibiotics can cure the plague. People must see a doctor fast. A vaccine exists, but the WHO only gives it to some workers. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S. Recently, a worker in Siberia died. The institute is now under quarantine.", "body_zh": "很久以前，鼠疫在欧洲夺走了很多人的生命。但它从未消失。今天，医生可以用药物治疗它。大多数病例发生在非洲。刚果和马达加斯加有很多病例。美国每年大约有七例。大多数发生在西部的农村地区。\n\n一种叫鼠疫耶尔森菌的细菌会引起鼠疫。老鼠和草原犬鼠等啮齿动物携带这种细菌。跳蚤叮咬这些动物，然后跳蚤再叮咬人。人们也可能因为接触生病的动物而生病。腺鼠疫是最常见的形式。它会让淋巴结非常疼痛和肿胀。\n\n还有另外两种形式。肺鼠疫感染肺部。败血型鼠疫感染血液。如果不尽早治疗，这两种都非常危险。如果人们不寻求帮助，腺鼠疫可能会变成这两种形式。肺鼠疫患者可以通过咳嗽传播疾病。\n\n抗生素可以治愈鼠疫。人们必须尽快去看医生。有疫苗，但世界卫生组织只给一些工作人员接种。美国没有鼠疫疫苗。最近，西伯利亚一名工作人员死亡。该研究所现在被隔离。", "text": "The plague killed many people in Europe long ago. It never went away. Today, doctors can treat it with medicine. Most cases are in Africa. Congo and Madagascar have many cases. The United States has about seven cases every year. Most are in the rural West. A bacteria called Yersinia pestis causes the plague. Rodents like rats and prairie dogs carry it. Fleas bite the animals. Then the fleas bite people. People can also get sick from sick animals. Bubonic plague is the most common form. It makes the lymph nodes very painful and swollen. There are two other forms. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs. Septicemic plague infects the blood. Both are very dangerous without early treatment. Bubonic plague can become these forms if people do not get help. A person with pneumonic plague can spread it by coughing. Antibiotics can cure the plague. People must see a doctor fast. A vaccine exists, but the WHO only gives it to some workers. There is no plague vaccine in the U.S. Recently, a worker in Siberia died. The institute is now under quarantine.", "vocab": [{"word": "plague", "pos": "noun", "zh": "鼠疫", "note": "一种严重的传染病"}, {"word": "bacteria", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细菌", "note": "复数形式，单数是 bacterium"}, {"word": "rodent", "pos": "noun", "zh": "啮齿动物", "note": "如老鼠、松鼠等"}, {"word": "flea", "pos": "noun", "zh": "跳蚤", "note": "一种会咬人的小虫子"}, {"word": "lymph node", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淋巴结", "note": "身体里的小豆状器官"}, {"word": "antibiotic", "pos": "noun", "zh": "抗生素", "note": "用来杀死细菌的药"}, {"word": "vaccine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "疫苗", "note": "用来预防疾病的注射剂"}, {"word": "quarantine", "pos": "noun", "zh": "隔离", "note": "为了防止疾病传播而限制人员流动"}], "title_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year", "summary_original": "The plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.", "body_original": "The plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year\nby The Associated Press\nedited by\nAndrew Zinin\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\nreputable news agency\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nThe plague, the rare bacterial infection infamously known for killing tens of millions in 14th-century Europe, never went away. It is now easily treated with antibiotics.\nSince the 1990s, most human cases have been reported in Africa, especially Congo and Madagascar, according to the\nWorld Health Organization\n.\nThere are an average of about seven human cases per year in the U.S.,\naccording to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\n, mostly in rural areas of the American West.\nA worker at a plague research institute in Siberia recently died of pneumonia.\nThe case has raised alarm\nin Russia after unconfirmed media reports suggested she might have died of pneumonic plague. Russian authorities say testing showed no evidence of plague, but the research institute has been quarantined.\n\"People think the plague is gone, it was something that occurred 500 years ago. It's not. It's here amongst us,\" said Simon Clarke of the University of Reading in the U.K. \"Unless you get rid of those wild animals, which of course is not possible, then you're not going to get rid of the bacteria.\"\nSurprised to hear the plague is still around? Here's what to know.\nPlague can attack lymph nodes, lungs and blood\nPeople in Siberia have been dying of plague for at least 5,500 years. The earliest evidence of a form of the disease was\nfound in teeth from ancient hunter-gatherers\nnorth of the region's Lake Baikal.\nPneumonic plague and the more well-known bubonic plague—also called the Black Death—are caused by the same bacteria, called Yersinia pestis.\nThe bacteria spread naturally among rodents like prairie dogs and rats. Fleas that jump from infected animals and bite people can spread the disease.\nThe bubonic plague is the most common form of the infection. It causes painfully swollen lymph nodes that are most commonly found in the groin, armpit and neck, called buboes. Other symptoms include sudden high fever and chills, headaches, and pain in the abdomen, legs and arms, according to the Cleveland Clinic.\nThere are two other forms of the plague: pneumonic plague, which can be caused by inhaling the bacteria, infects the lungs, while septicemic plague is an infection in the bloodstream. Both are fatal if not treated early, according to the World Health Organization.\nBubonic plague will often develop into the other two forms of plague if untreated.\nPlague can spread through fleas or bodily fluids\nOther than bites from infected fleas, people can also get plague by touching infected bodily fluids, so health experts recommend taking extra care when handling dead or sick animals.\nThe plague can also spread through the respiratory droplets of someone who has pneumonic plague.\n\"If you cough, you can spread it to another person, but that doesn't travel very far,\" said Dr. Glenn Wortmann, MedStar Washington Hospital Center. \"So you really need to be in face-to-face contact with that person to get pneumonic plague.\"\nThat's why \"the chance of this spreading widely in Russia and then extending here to the U.S. will not happen,\" he said.\nAntibiotics can treat plague\nThe plague was never eradicated, but we've gotten better at preventing its spread and treating it in humans.\nWhen treated early with antibiotics, the plague can be cured. The key is to get to a doctor fast—otherwise the plague can be deadly.\nThere is a vaccine, but the WHO only recommends it for people who are at high risk of infection, like laboratory and healthcare workers. There isn't strong evidence that vaccines can work against the pneumonic form.\nThere's no plague vaccine available in the U.S.\nKey medical concepts\nBubonic Plague\nPneumonic Plague\nYersinia pestis\nAntibiotics\nClinical categories\nInfectious diseases\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nWho's behind this story?\nAndrew Zinin\nMaster's in physics with research experience. Long-time science news enthusiast. Plays key role in Science X's editorial success.\nFull profile →\n© 2026 The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten or redistributed without permission.\nCitation\n:\nThe plague never went away. Cases are reported in parts of Africa and the US nearly every year (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-plague-cases-africa-year.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T09:31:25.521123", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "The", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.275, "sm": 100, "em": 275, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "plague", "s": 0.287, "e": 0.6, "sm": 287, "em": 600, "cs": 4, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "killed", "s": 0.613, "e": 0.925, "sm": 612, "em": 925, "cs": 11, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "many", "s": 0.938, "e": 1.2, "sm": 937, "em": 1200, "cs": 18, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "people", "s": 1.212, "e": 1.562, "sm": 1212, "em": 1562, "cs": 23, "ce": 29, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "in", "s": 1.575, "e": 1.662, "sm": 1575, "em": 1662, "cs": 30, "ce": 32, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "Europe", "s": 1.675, "e": 2.038, "sm": 1675, "em": 2037, "cs": 33, "ce": 39, "